Closes #91284

AI-assisted: yes (Codex). I reviewed and understand the change.

## What Problem This Solves

An exposed Gateway using complete trusted-proxy authentication was reported by `openclaw doctor` as “without authentication.” Doctor then recommended generating a token, even though token auth is mutually exclusive with trusted-proxy mode.

That false critical is confusing for reverse-proxy deployments and can steer operators away from the security boundary they intentionally configured.

## Why This Change Was Made

Doctor now recognizes trusted-proxy authentication only when the configuration can satisfy the Gateway’s existing startup and request-authorization contracts. The implementation reuses the runtime owners—resolved auth, startup validation, HTTP authorization, source checks, and browser-origin matching—instead of creating a second permissive auth policy inside doctor.

The downgrade remains deliberately fail-closed:

- every trusted proxy source must be a canonical, usable, host-scoped address: exact IP, `/32`, or `/128`;
- subnets, default routes, invalid/non-unicast entries, disallowed loopback, host-interface sources, and interface-inspection failures remain `CRITICAL`;
- missing/invalid identity headers, unusable `allowUsers`, and actual shared-token conflicts remain `CRITICAL`;
- wildcard origins, dangerous Host-header origin fallback, and missing/nonmatchable explicit browser origins remain independent `CRITICAL` findings;
- independent proxy and browser-origin defects are reported together in one doctor run.

An inactive token SecretRef remains inactive, matching Gateway startup behavior; plaintext or environment-derived token conflicts still fail closed.

This is a doctor classification/remediation change only. It does not change runtime authentication, config/schema, protocol behavior, defaults, or stored data.

## User Impact

Operators with a complete host-scoped trusted-proxy deployment now receive an accurate exposure warning with proxy/firewall guidance instead of an unauthenticated critical and token-repair advice. Unsafe or incomplete deployments keep specific critical findings.

Scope metrics:

- config/default surfaces added, changed, or removed: 0;
- migrations or stored-data changes: 0;
- runtime auth behavior changes: 0;
- diagnostic classification/remediation changes: 1 bounded surface.

## Evidence

Exact control base (canonical `main` at rebase): `87de81a5fd4a2d4e3ef8bff9af22a3ed2ab8b4e8`  
Exact patched head: `0a526e46e7064ed1ec168097139f6e041b1195c0`

A synthetic before/after VHS recording shows:

- control red: the exact base misreports the LAN-bound Gateway as unauthenticated and recommends token repair;
- fixed: the exact head recognizes trusted-proxy authentication and retains network-exposure/firewall guidance;
- focused proof: 237 tests pass across five files and two Vitest shards (141 Gateway + 96 doctor).

The 1400×900 recording uses only RFC 5737 test data, `x-forwarded-user`, and `https://control.example.test`. All 696 MP4 frames and 418 GIF frames decoded successfully and were timing/signal/privacy audited. No local paths, personal identifiers, credentials, private-network details, or tokens are visible.

- MP4 SHA-256: `1f1843e4c4d19feab121c5027a2e00b910b275de2ad104ea0d7bb414ae65b3f2`
- GIF SHA-256: `aaeef92cfb748b105008e957ef949da038cccd8bdd5c026ffe75d55c0966dcc7`

Exact-head verification:

- focused runtime/doctor tests: 237/237 passed;
- changed-surface gate: passed formatting, lint, core/core-test typechecks, dependency/API/plugin-boundary guards, database-first/media/sidecar guards, webhook/pairing guards, and zero runtime import cycles;
- commit-mode autoreview: clean, no actionable findings; verdict “patch is correct”;
- source trust: maintained contributor branch, verified exact Git objects, clean worktree.

Boundary: the recording proves doctor CLI classification/remediation and the focused runtime contracts. It does not claim a live remote reverse-proxy/browser login flow.

Origin-check findings are intentionally trusted-proxy-only: proxy auth is ambient (header-based), making the Origin allowlist the CSRF boundary; token/password modes require explicit per-request credentials. A doctor origin audit for those modes would be a separate follow-up.

## Summary

- add dynamic configured-model choices to the native `/model` command
- enable the existing command argument menu path for `/model` so Slack can render model picks with its existing buttons/static_select/external_select machinery
- keep bare `/model` status behavior when no configured model choices are available

## Details

`/think` already gets rich Slack command UI because its registry entry declares choices plus an argument menu. `/model` had a positional argument but no choices/menu metadata, so Slack had nothing to render before dispatching the command as plain text.

This change keeps the fix in the shared command registry: configured catalog entries become `provider/model` choices, and the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter handles the selected value. No Slack-specific command renderer changes are needed.

## Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/auto-reply/commands-registry.test.ts`
- `git diff --check HEAD~1..HEAD`
- `node --import tsx - <<'EOF' ...` registry smoke shown below

## Real behavior proof

Behavior addressed: Slack native slash-command UX for `/model` can now surface the existing command argument menu instead of requiring users to type a model id manually.

Real environment tested: local OpenClaw source checkout in an isolated worktree from `origin/main`, running the actual command registry module after this patch.

Exact steps or command run after this patch: ran this local terminal command against the patched checkout:

```bash
node --import tsx - <<'EOF'
import { findCommandByNativeName, resolveCommandArgMenu } from './src/auto-reply/commands-registry.ts';
const command = findCommandByNativeName('model');
if (!command) throw new Error('missing /model command');
const menu = resolveCommandArgMenu({
  command,
  cfg: {
    models: {
      providers: {
        openai: { models: [{ id: 'gpt-5.5', name: 'GPT-5.5' }] },
        anthropic: { models: [{ id: 'claude-sonnet-4.6', name: 'Claude Sonnet 4.6' }] },
      },
    },
  },
});
console.log(JSON.stringify({ arg: menu?.arg.name, choices: menu?.choices }, null, 2));
EOF
```

Evidence after fix: terminal output from the patched OpenClaw registry smoke:

```json
{
  "arg": "model",
  "choices": [
    {
      "value": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6",
      "label": "anthropic/Claude Sonnet 4.6"
    },
    {
      "value": "openai/gpt-5.5",
      "label": "openai/GPT-5.5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Observed result after fix: `/model` now resolves a native command argument menu for configured model choices, using `provider/model` values that the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter dispatch path already accepts. Focused registry tests also passed 31/31.

What was not tested: live Slack Block Kit interaction against a real Slack workspace. The Slack plugin path reused here is already covered by existing arg-menu render/dispatch tests; this PR adds the missing registry metadata that feeds that path.

## Notes

AI-assisted with human-requested agent workflow. The local commit helper hydrated ignored `node_modules` in the isolated worktree before committing; no tracked dependency or lockfile changes are included.

